         DIGITAL'S ALPHA AXP SYSTEM POWERS VIRTUAL REALITY
       ENTERTAINMENT SYSTEM WITH WIDESPREAD COMMERCIAL APPEAL

        
MAYNARD, Mass. -- June 29, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation's 
Alpha AXP computers have been chosen as the base for a commercially 
available entertainment system offering interactive, real-time, 3D 
virtual reality capabilities.  SyberTrek AG of Zurich, Switzerland 
has developed the SyberLine One system based on the Digital Alpha 
AXP workstation. The DEC 3000 Model 600 Alpha AXP workstation 
running the DEC OSF/1 operating system is equipped with two 3D 
graphics accelerators from Kubota Graphics Corporation.

     SyberLine One debuted June 15, 1994 at Alcatel STR's Virtual 
Reality Symposium in Zurich's Technologypark. 			   

     Juergen Brockmann, CEO of Merkur Spielothek, stated after 
flying through the Syberspace, "This is the most advanced 
Virtual-Reality technique that is available today. Accordingly, we 
are evaluating this product for our centers." Merkur Spielothek is 
Germany's leading arcade organization with 140 centers in that 
country, as well as operations in the U.K., Netherlands, Czech 
Republic and Slovakian Republic. 

     The SyberLine One player's helmet displays interactive 3D 
impressions through two small eye-level monitors. Head movements are 
transmitted via helmet sensors, thus altering the image seen by the 
player. A joy stick creates the sensation of flight, while an audio 
system provides stereo sound.

     The flight game "On the Edge of the Universe - Watchdog 7" was 
demonstrated at the Virtual Reality Symposium. The game allows 
players to fly through canyons, ward off enemies in the air and 
defend space stations. Since the systems are linked, participants 
either play against the computer or against each other.

     SyberTrek AG, the technology leader in Virtual Reality 
Simulation Systems, is also exploring industrial market 
applications such as flight simulators for the system.

     Merkur Spielothek is part of the Gauselmann-Group, the largest 
coin operated amusement machine producer in Europe with 3500 
employees and 1993 revenues of 917 million DM. 

     Kubota Graphics Corporation develops, manufactures and markets 
high-performance 3D graphics and image processing workstations and 
graphics accelerators for design, engineering, technical, and 
entertainment applications.  Headquartered in Santa Clara, 
California, Kubota Graphics is a wholly-owned subsidiary of Kubota 
Corporation, a $7 billion multinational corporation.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's Alpha AXP platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with 
industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace
