                   DIGITAL EQUIPMENT CORPORATION

                             FACT SHEET


PRODUCT NAME:  DCE for OpenVMS FACT SHEET


OVERVIEW:  DIGITAL'S DCE LEADERSHIP

This announcement extends Digital's leadership in delivering DCE 
products. OpenVMS systems now provide full DCE core services which 
enable secure interactions among diverse users, applications and 
services, regardless of where they reside. Thus, users and programs 
can access resources without knowing their location and 
unauthorized access can be prevented, in distributed multivendor 
environments.  

Digital began delivery of DCE for OpenVMS in August, 1993, with a 
developer's kit for OpenVMS VAX users.  The developer's kit  
provided Remote Procedure Call (RPC), DCE threads, and the IDL 
compiler.  It enabled developers to write applications, using an 
easy-to-use, ANSI C-based language for writing remote procedure 
calls, which, for example, enables OpenVMS  servers and Windows NT 
clients to interoperate using Digital and Microsoft RPC.

Next, Digital extended DCE for OpenVMS support to the Alpha AXP 
platform and delivered Distributed Time Service (DTS) and  client 
support for Cell Directory Service (CDS) and for DCE Security  
Service.  These capabilities enable OpenVMS DCE-based applications 
to interoperate with DEC OSF/1 and all other vendors' OSF-certified  
DCE implementations.   
 
The DCE Security Server and DCE Cell Directory Server, that began 
shipping on June 15, 1994, complete the delivery of full DCE core 
services. 


TECHNICAL DESCRIPTION:

Full DCE core services consist of threads, RPCs, time services, 
directory services, and security services.  

DCE Threads - supports the creation, management, and 
synchronization of multiple threads of control within a single 
process.  Threads can independently carry out sequential processing 
of different parts of an application.  Because a server process 
using threads can handle many clients simultaneously, the Threads 
service is ideally suited to deal with multiple clients in a 
client/server-based application.

Remote Procedure Call (RPC) - is a development tool and a runtime 
service. RPC allows individual procedures of an application to run 
on systems anywhere on the network.  It extends the typical local 
procedure call model by supporting direct calls to procedures on 
remote systems, enabling programmers to develop distributed 
applications as easily as traditional, single-system applications.  
RPC shields programmers from the complexities of the underlying 
networking technologies and masks the differences between data 
representations on different systems, e.g. character 
representations and integer byte ordering.

Distributed Time Service (DTS) - provides synchronized system time 
for all the nodes running in a DCE environment.  Many distributed 
applications need a single time reference to schedule activities 
and determine event sequencing and duration across networks of 
computers in a distributed environment.

Directory Service - allows access to resources by a single name, 
regardless of physical location.  It provides a consistent way to 
locate people and resources without knowing their physical 
location.  The Cell Directory Server (CDS) provides a database of 
DCE cell resources such as users, servers, files and systems within 
a DCE cell.  The Global Directory Agent provides a means of 
linking multiple CDS namespaces.

Security Service - protects resources from illegal access and 
provides secure communications within and between DCE cells.  It 
verifies the identity of a DCE user or service, controls access to 
resources, verifies data integrity and ensures data privacy. DCE 
Security Service enables secure communications and controlled 
access to resources in a distributed computing environment.
                                ####

