            DIGITAL EQUIPMENT CORPORATION TO HONOR NAACP
                         ACADEMIC OLYMPIANS

     
       Fifteen gold medalists will receive personal computers
                          and printers...


CHICAGO, Illinois -- July 11, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
will honor some of America's most talented minority youth tonight, 
with awards of personal computers, printers and a special computer 
science prize to gold medalists of the NAACP's 1994 Afro-Academic, 
Cultural, Technological and Scientific Olympics (ACT-SO).  Digital 
has supported the ACT-SO awards since 1983 and is a major corporate 
sponsor.

     "Through its ongoing support of ACT-SO, Digital is helping
encourage and inspire black youth to strive for excellence in 
academic and cultural pursuits," said Dr. Benjamin Chavis, 
Executive Director of the NAACP.  "The company's long standing 
contribution to ACT-SO confirms that Digital truly recognizes the 
value of youth development to America's future." 

     The ACT-SO program is an annual nationwide academic 
competition for students in grades nine through twelve. For the 
last three days, finalists from local competitions at NAACP 
branches across the country have engaged in intense competition for 
top honors in 24 categories, including the humanities, sciences, 
performing and visual arts.

     "At Digital, we are committed to youth development and to
fostering excellence both inside and outside the classroom," said 
Scott Roeth, Vice President of Digital's U.S. Territory. "We are 
pleased to continue our support of the NAACP and its ACT-SO 
program. We feel that this program truly encourages thousands of 
students each year to reach higher and pursue their dreams." 

     Gold medalists in 15 categories will receive Digital personal 
computers and printers.  Digital will also present a $5,000 
computer science award and will contribute $1,000 to the 
organization sponsoring each winner.  In addition, Digital has 
automated registration for the NAACP convention, which began here 
yesterday. 

     Digital will present the awards at a gala reception it is
hosting this evening at the University of Chicago Graduate School 
of Business.  Carl Chanson Williams, Vice President of Information 
Technology Worldwide for the AMOCO Corporation, will address the 
group on the importance of higher education for minorities. 
Renowned jazz musician Thelonius Monk, Jr., will be a featured 
guest at the reception, which will include performances by ACT-SO
students. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation also supports youth development 
through its Kids Initiatives with Digital Support (K.I.D.S.) 
program.  K.I.D.S. provides grants for children and their families 
in communities across the country. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace. 
