             IRISH AEROSPACE COMPANY CHOOSES DIGITAL'S
       Alpha AXP SYSTEMS TO BOOST AIRCRAFT DESIGN CAPABILITY


MAYNARD, Mass. -- July 12, 1994 -- Short Brothers, the Belfast, 
Ireland-based aerospace company and division of the Bombardier 
Group of Montreal, Canada, has invested in Digital Equipment 
Corporation's Alpha AXP 64-bit computing platform to significantly 
boost the efficiency of a critical component of its operations. DEC 
3000 Alpha AXP computers running the DEC OSF/1 operating system 
will be used in computational fluid dynamics (CFD), the discipline 
primarily concerned with aerodynamic design of aircraft shapes.

     Based on preliminary results, the Alpha AXP systems are 
achieving approximately one third the performance of a Cray 2 or a 
Cray YMP-EL supercomputer at 1/20th to 1/30th the price.  In 
addition, Alpha AXP systems offer Short Brothers substantially 
lower maintenance costs. 

     According to James Mathews, Assistant Chief Aerodynamicist 
with Short Brothers, "We have to be conscious of the exchange 
between speed and accuracy of flow prediction.  With Alpha AXP, 
more sophisticated and accurate methods can be used during the 
aerodynamic design process.  This generates greater confidence 
without adversely affecting the design process time."

     "The use of Alpha AXP has reduced the analysis times by 
factors of between three and six.  We can therefore examine many 
more cases, which is important because we should not optimize an 
aircraft for a single flight condition; we have to ensure the 
aerodynamic design operates efficiently over a range of flight 
conditions," added Mathews.

     In association with the UK Defence Research Agency, Short 
Brothers is intending to develop additional state-of-the-art 
computational fluid dynamics applications.  			   

