           MAJOR U.K. UTILITY MOVES TO Alpha AXP PLATFORM


MAYNARD, Mass. -- July 22, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation's 
64-bit Alpha AXP technology has been chosen to support the 
U.K.-based Southern Electric's Engineering Systems Project. The 
project is valued at approximately $5.2 million (US) in sales to 
Digital.

     Southern Electric's choice is the latest in a stream of recent 
decisions made by utility companies throughout the United Kingdom 
in favor of Alpha AXP systems, including Northern Electric, 
Yorkshire Electricity, SEEBOARD, London Electricity and National 
Grid Company for the industry wide Settlement System.

     Measured against the key criteria of performance, scalability, 
cost-of-ownership and application availability, Southern Electric 
decided to purchase 80 Alpha AXP workstations and 40 Alpha AXP 
servers running both the OpenVMS and DEC OSF/1 operating systems, 
Digital's implementation of the UNIX operating system.  The 
workstations will support applications such as GIS (from 
SmallWorld), Network Distribution Management and Power System 
Modeling.  The servers will support both the GIS project and the 
corporate plant and circuit database.

     Matt Devereux, Southern Electric's Director of IT commented, 
"In addition to the power of the technology, we also see our 
commitment to the Alpha AXP platform as a key element in Southern 
Electric's drive towards the building of an open IT environment."

     "Performance will clearly be key for a system such as 
Southern's Engineering Program," added Tim Dyson, Digital's 
Electricity Supply Industry (ESI) Sales Manager. "But equally 
important is the longevity of the design architecture and the 
exceptional cost-of-ownership of the Alpha AXP technology."

     Digital has been working with Southern Electric for the last 
two years, designing and building IT systems which support their 
goal of providing the highest levels of customer service at the 
lowest costs in the industry.

     The Engineering Systems Project program has seven key 
elements, with the plant and circuit asset register (PLACAR) 
database at the heart of the system.  Developed by Digital, the 
database will provide vital information about the network and 
ensure that a consistent record is kept of all the company's key 
assets.  Access to this information will be available through a 
GUI-based front end.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
