          DIGITAL ANNOUNCES ChannelWorks INTERNET BROUTER
            FOR HIGH-SPEED INTERNET ACCESS VIA CABLE TV


MAYNARD, Mass. -- July 25, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the introduction of the ChannelWorks Internet 
Brouter, a unique product in the industry which allows businesses 
high-speed access to the Internet over standard cable television 
systems.  Based on the ChannelWorks Bridge, which delivers 10
megabits per second Ethernet connectivity over the cable television 
infrastructure, the ChannelWorks Internet Brouter provides access 
to and routing of Internet traffic at speeds significantly greater 
than those of traditional leased lines. The physical link to the 
Internet is provided by local cable television operators, who 
contract with Internet service providers. 

     "The high data transfer rates of cable television are ideal 
for quickly retrieving and sending applications such as video and 
multimedia which are key to the growth of the information 
superhighway," said Dr. Laurence Walker, vice president and general 
manager of Digital's Network Product Business.  "The ChannelWorks 
Internet Brouter provides a high bandwidth extention of the
Ethernet into cable TV networks.  As the Internet grows, this 
capability is becoming increasingly important to our customers."  

     The Internet is rapidly becoming an important tool for 
business, in addition to its already widespread use in universities 
and government. The Internet is a global public network with over 
25 million users, and is doubling each year in terms of users, 
networks, hosts and data traffic.  The Internet allows users to
communicate with each other via worldwide electronic mail, as well 
as more data-intensive applications such as accessing worldwide 
databases and information repositories like the World-Wide Web.  
This type of data-intensive access to information is currently 
provided by expensive, high-speed leased lines.  The ChannelWorks 
Internet Brouter provides inexpensive connectivity to these 
resources at higher speeds than leased lines.  

ChannelWorks Goes to School

     Important applications of the ChannelWorks technology are 
already being tested. The National Science Foundation is funding a 
collaboration between the University of Pittsburgh, the Pittsburgh 
Public Schools and the Pittsburgh Supercomputing Center called 
"Common Knowledge: Pittsburgh."  Digital and TCI serve as 
affiliates to the project; Digital's ChannelWorks product will 
deliver access to the Internet via TCI's cable system.  "Common 
Knowledge: Pittsburgh" is one of four projects in the nation 
designated by the NSF as a testbed for school networking.  

     Professor of Physics Robert Carlitz, at the University of 
Pittsburgh, is a principal investigator for the "Common Knowledge: 
Pittsburgh" project.  Carlitz states, "Digital's ChannelWorks 
technology offers what may be an optimal solution to school 
connectivity, with the promise of high bandwidth at moderate 
prices. "The same technology is of considerable interest for 
commercial applications, so it is a candidate for very widespread 
deployment."

     "If you think of your average telephone bill and your average 
cable bill, they are roughly the same," added Carlitz.  "But, with 
cable, you have the capacity for 10,000,000 bits per second of raw 
bandwidth, as compared to 28,800 bits per second for data 
transmission over an analog phone line."

Benefits for the MSOs

     "The ChannelWorks Internet Brouter also provides new revenue 
streams for the cable TV industry's multiple systems operators 
(MSOs)," said Zins.  "This allows the cable companies to expand 
their reach beyond traditional entertainment markets and begin to 
build the highway for data and information interchange."

     As regulatory rate restrictions constrain the cable operators' 
current businesses, there is a need to find new opportunities for 
revenue.  Cable companies are joining with Digital to provide new 
services, such as Internet access, to a new customer base.

How it Works

     The ChannelWorks Internet Brouter provides TCP/IP routing over 
cable TV systems and supports the simple network management 
protocol (SNMP) standard to facilitate smooth-running network 
operations and troubleshooting.

     The Brouter routes data transmissions over existing cable 
channels and runs on any cable plant with two-way transmission. It 
uses one 6 MHz forward channel for outbound transmissions, and one 
6 MHz reverse channel for inbound transmissions.  These data 
transmissions run alongside other programming channels, and multiple 
customers can use the network without worrying about information 
security, because the Brouter's network management allows 
administrators to create separate logical networks for various user 
groups.

     Users install a ChannelWorks Internet Brouter at each LAN 
site, with one TransMaster translator at the cable company's 
headend, the hub of the cable television system.  The local cable 
television operators contract with Internet service providers for 
the physical link to the Internet.

Pricing, Availability

     The ChannelWorks Internet Brouter will be available in the 
U.S. within the next 60 days and will be distributed worldwide 
later this year.  The product has a list price of $6,995 and is 
available through Digital's authorized resellers and distributors. 

     A leader in networking, Digital continues to work with the 
cable TV industry's multiple systems operators to develop new 
solutions for high-speed, broadband data communications over cable.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
