          DIGITAL'S SEMICONDUCTOR BUSINESS UNIT ANNOUNCES
           NEW SALES STRUCTURE, REAFFIRMS MERCHANT ROLE

       
               Expands Sales Channels for Alpha AXP
             Microprocessors and PCI Peripheral Chips
       

MAYNARD, Mass. -- July 25, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today reaffirmed its merchant chip vendor role and revealed a new 
sales structure for its Semiconductor Business Unit, a global 
organization announced last week to serve the worldwide semiconductor 
market.

     The Semiconductor Business Unit is responsible for designing,
manufacturing, marketing, and selling Digital's semiconductor
products, including Alpha AXP microprocessors and value-added
Peripheral Component Interconnect (PCI) chips.

     "We have developed significant momentum as a merchant vendor 
with our leading-edge semiconductor products," said Ed Caldwell, 
vice president, Semiconductor Business Unit. "Becoming a business
unit is a recognition of that achievement. Combined with Digital's
strength and market presence, it gives us new leverage and
flexibility to build our business."

Technology, Performance Leadership

     The Digital Semiconductor Business Unit's worldwide operations
encompass chip fabrication facilities in Hudson, Mass., and South
Queensferry, Scotland; design centers in Hudson, Palo Alto, Calif.,
Austin, Tex., and Jerusalem, Israel; and sales and marketing
management at its Hudson headquarters. A new, $450-million
semiconductor fabrication facility is nearing completion in Hudson
to enable production of future-generation chips with
sub-half-micron features. To complement Digital's fabrication
capability, Mitsubishi Electric Corporation was named in March 1993
as a second source for Alpha AXP microprocessors.

     "We intend to make the Alpha AXP architecture an open market 
standard and to continue our technology leadership. Alpha AXP 
microprocessors have maintained performance superiority since their
introduction more than two years ago -- a remarkable feat in this
age of high-technology leapfrog," said Caldwell. "We were also a
founder of the PCI Special Interest Group and made an early
commitment to PCI technology. Our goal is to sustain both our
microprocessor and our PCI leadership."

Worldwide Sales Strategy

     The Semiconductor Business Unit has implemented a worldwide 
semiconductor sales strategy under the leadership of Arthur Swift,
recently appointed vice president, Semiconductor Marketing and
Sales, and Richard Riker, new worldwide sales manager. This global
sales effort, focusing on North America, Europe, and Asia, will be
led by Digital area sales managers and field application engineers,
and will include manufacturers' representatives and distributors in
these major world markets.

     Area sales managers and manufacturers' representatives are 
currently being chosen for worldwide geographies. Initial 
selections for U.S. manufacturers' representatives include: 
Premier Technical Sales, Inc., Northern California; Vision 
Technical Sales, Inc., Los Angeles/Orange County, Calif.; Silicon
Technical Sales, Inc., San Diego, Calif.; Quad States Sales and
Marketing, Inc., Texas; and Genesis Associates, Inc., Northeast.

     Distribution agreements were announced last December with 
Hamilton Hallmark division of Avnet, Inc., Pioneer-Standard 
Electronics, and Wyle Laboratories.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
