              DIGITAL PREVIEWS ADVANCED DEVELOPMENT ENHANCEMENTS
                  TO COMMON OBJECT MODEL AT OBJECT WORLD '94

         
      ... Technology provides advanced object development capabilities...
    ... Enables creation and use of COM objects across object languages...
    
         
SAN FRANCISCO, Calif. -- July 27, 1994 -- This week, Digital 
Equipment Corporation is debuting advanced object technology designed
to meet customer demand for a robust development environment for the
Common Object Model (COM) architecture. Known by the internal name
"Pegasus," the new technology improves developers' productivity by
layering powerful class structures, bass class libraries, and
cross-language sharing on COM. Notably, Pegasus will enable
COM-compliant applications to share objects and classes with
applications based on popular object languages such as VisualWorks,
NeXT, Forte, and C++.
         
     The extensions to the basic COM environment -- Digital and 
Microsoft's strategic object model and environment for integrating the
desktop with the enterprise -- will be available from Digital in an
advanced development kit in early 1995, and will later be integrated
into Digital's ObjectBroker products.
     
     "These advanced object development capabilities are a key addition
to Digital's open client/server strategy," said Steve Jenkins, senior
group manager for Digital's Enterprise Objects Software Group. "Our
goal with the Enterprise Objects Software Program is to reduce our
customers' application development and maintenance costs through object 
technology. ObjectBroker -- and soon Pegasus -- accomplishes this goal
by significantly enhancing developers' options, whether they are
integrating a loosely coupled desktop environment, managing a highly
complex development environment, or handling a combination of the two."
         
The advanced development kit planned for early 1995 will include:
         
  o class structures to improve development quality and 
    productivity by providing inheritance; 
    
  o standard programming objects need by most OO developers.  
    Commonly referred to as base class libraries, these objects 
    include streams, files, and namespaces; and

  o cross language sharing to enable developers to create and 
    use COM objects and classes across languages.  In addition 
    to improving productivity, this feature creates a more open 
    environment by freeing developers to choose the best 
    language for a project rather than compromise on a single 
    language or development tool set. 
         
Booth Highlights
         
     The Pegasus demonstration in the Digital booth #501 creates a 
banking environment in which objects are shared both within a 
heterogeneous environment and between previously incompatible object
development environments. The demonstration includes ObjectBroker,
COM, Pegasus, Forte, VisualWorks, and C++ products running across
SunOS, IBM OS/2, HP-UX, OSF/1 AXP, Windows on Intel, and NT AXP
platforms.  Also prominent in the booth are demonstrations of the
ObjectBroker OLE Network Portal feature, which allows currently
available shrink-wrapped applications that support Windows 3.1 and OLE 
(e.g., Microsoft Word and Excel, Lotus 1-2-3, WordPerfect) to access
information on UNIX and OpenVMS servers via ObjectBroker; and the
recently announced port of NeXT's PDO system to Digital's
high-performance AXP systems.

     Digital is this year's sponsor and host for the popular OMG OO
Award Reception on Wednesday, July 27 at 5:00 in the main hall. 
Everyone attending the show is invited to this celebration of the best
new OO products.
         
ObjectBroker and COM
         
     ObjectBroker is Digital's layered software product that helps 
developers create distributed client/server solutions by simplifying
the task of integrating existing applications and developing new
applications in mixed vendor environments. Fully compliant with the
Object Management Group's CORBA standard, ObjectBroker is shipping
today on the broadest range of platforms in the industry, including
Windows, Windows NT, Macintosh, OpenVMS, DEC OSF/1, HP-UX, IBM AIX, 
SunOS, and Ultrix. 
         
     Digital and Microsoft announced the COM specification in November
1993 as a joint development effort to create a single object model to
integrate the desktop with the enterprise. The specification allows
the companies' leading object technologies, ObjectBroker and Object
Linking and Embedding (OLE), to work together. The companies recently 
hosted a technical preview of the architecture which was attended by
representatives of more than 100 companies and industry groups.
         
     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide
information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha AXP platforms, storage,
networking, software and services, together with industry-focused
solutions from business partners, help organizations compete and win in
today's global marketplace.
